WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.452
A web crawler starts at one

2
00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:06.190
page and follows links.

3
00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:09.500
It wants every reachable page, once.

4
00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:12.760
If it simply follows every link it sees

5
00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:15.420
it will go round in circles forever

6
00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:18.650
page A links to B, B links back to A.

7
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.970
So it must remember where it has been.

8
00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:24.850
And it must decide, at each moment

9
00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:26.720
which page to explore next.

10
00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:29.705
That one decision, which page next

11
00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:30.921
is the difference between the

12
00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:32.790
two famous graph searches.

13
00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:35.515
This video is about breadth-first

14
00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:37.770
search and depth-first search.

15
00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:40.710
We'll see the frontier idea they share

16
00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:43.110
how a queue gives breadth-first search

17
00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:46.030
and how a stack, or recursion

18
00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:47.960
gives depth-first search.

19
00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.670
Every search keeps three kinds of vertices.

20
00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:52.970
Undiscovered ones.

21
00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:53.990
Explored ones

22
00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:56.270
whose neighbours we have all looked at.

23
00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:58.570
And the frontier: discovered

24
00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:00.380
but not yet explored.

25
00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:02.640
The loop is always the same.

26
00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.560
Take a vertex off the frontier.

27
00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:05.923
Look at its neighbours.

28
00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:07.726
Any neighbour we have never seen

29
00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:09.730
goes onto the frontier.

30
00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.332
The only free choice is which

31
00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:13.750
vertex to take off.

32
00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:15.010
Take the oldest

33
00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:16.870
and the frontier is a queue.

34
00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:19.880
Take the newest, and it is a stack.

35
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:22.710
Breadth-first search, from A

36
00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:23.950
with the queue.

37
00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:25.190
Take A off.

38
00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:28.060
Its neighbours, B and D, go on the queue.

39
00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:29.720
Take B off.

40
00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:33.640
Its neighbours are A, C, D and E.

41
00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:36.080
A and D are already known.

42
00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:39.010
C and E are new, so they join the queue.

43
00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:40.890
Take D off.

44
00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:42.270
Only F is new.

45
00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:44.550
Then C: nothing new.

46
00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:47.410
Then E: G is new.

47
00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:49.320
Then F, then G.

48
00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:57.240
The order of visits: A, B, D, C, E, F, G.

49
00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:58.980
Count the edges from A

50
00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:01.780
B and D are one step away, C

51
00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:04.710
E and F are two, and G is three.

52
00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:07.990
Breadth-first search explores in rings

53
00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:10.370
first everything one edge from A

54
00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:13.020
then everything two edges away, and so on.

55
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.358
One detail matters.

56
00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:18.483
We mark a vertex as seen the moment

57
00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:19.890
we put it on the queue

58
00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:21.480
not when we take it off.

59
00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:23.263
Otherwise a vertex can be

60
00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:25.040
queued again and again

61
00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:27.070
by every neighbour that reaches it.

62
00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.190
Try it on a triangle.

63
00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:33.490
A is connected to B and to C, and B to C.

64
00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:35.050
Without marking

65
00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:37.450
every pop adds two more vertices.

66
00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:39.110
After twenty pops

67
00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:41.410
the queue is longer than when we began

68
00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:43.330
A has been visited seven times

69
00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:45.620
and the search never ends.

70
00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.790
Each vertex remembers who discovered it

71
00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:49.990
its parent.

72
00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:54.850
Follow the parents back from G: E, then B

73
00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:56.040
then A.

74
00:02:56.390 --> 00:03:01.980
So the route from A to G is A, B, E, G.

75
00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:03.620
Three edges.

76
00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:05.219
No other route has fewer

77
00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:08.078
because breadth-first search reaches a vertex

78
00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:10.590
in the first ring that touches it.

79
00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:12.500
Pause and predict.

80
00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:14.340
Our edges have weights.

81
00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:16.860
Is the route A, B, E

82
00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.810
G also the cheapest route from A to G?

83
00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:23.970
Add it up: six, plus seven, plus ten.

84
00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:25.160
Twenty-three.

85
00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:27.627
But there is a route with the same three

86
00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:33.100
edges that costs only twenty: A, D, F, G.

87
00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:35.710
Three, plus five, plus twelve.

88
00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:38.590
Breadth-first search counts edges.

89
00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:40.465
It does not look at weights.

90
00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:42.227
For cheapest routes we need

91
00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:43.390
another algorithm

92
00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:45.100
and that is the next video.

93
00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.490
Now take the newest vertex instead.

94
00:03:48.490 --> 00:03:50.610
Depth-first search, from A.

95
00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:53.270
We go to A's first neighbour, B

96
00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:54.480
and keep going.

97
00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:56.920
From B, to C.

98
00:03:56.920 --> 00:03:58.400
From C, to E.

99
00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:00.340
From E, to D.

100
00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:01.780
From D, to F.

101
00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:03.721
From F, to G.

102
00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:06.194
We always continue from the

103
00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:08.230
vertex we just found.

104
00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:11.090
G has no unseen neighbour.

105
00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:16.190
We are stuck, so we back up to F, then D

106
00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:19.250
then E, all the way back to A

107
00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:21.980
and check each one for anything we skipped.

108
00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:30.930
The order of discovery: A, B, C, E, D, F, G.

109
00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:34.575
Depth-first search can keep a clock.

110
00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:36.002
Write down the time you

111
00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:37.560
first reach a vertex

112
00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:39.740
and the time you finish with it

113
00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:42.070
when every neighbour has been handled.

114
00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:45.010
A is discovered at time one

115
00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:47.745
and finished last, at fourteen.

116
00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:50.307
G is discovered at seven

117
00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:52.540
and finished at eight, right away.

118
00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:53.952
Look at the intervals.

119
00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.890
Each vertex's interval sits

120
00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:57.340
inside its parent's.

121
00:04:57.340 --> 00:04:59.920
They are nested, like brackets.

122
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.765
That nesting is what depth-first

123
00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:03.130
search computes.

124
00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:06.700
Depth-first search is naturally recursive.

125
00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:09.100
To visit a vertex, mark it

126
00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:11.470
and then visit each unseen neighbour.

127
00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:15.190
The call stack is the stack of the search.

128
00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:18.770
Each call is a vertex still being explored.

129
00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:20.940
Backing up is just returning.

130
00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:24.340
You can do the same with an explicit stack

131
00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:26.480
push the neighbours in reverse order

132
00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:28.460
and pop the next vertex.

133
00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:31.330
It visits in exactly the same order.

134
00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:34.520
Depth-first search also finds cycles.

135
00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:36.037
In an undirected graph

136
00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:38.292
a cycle shows up as an edge to a

137
00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:40.220
vertex we have already seen

138
00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:42.270
other than the one we came from.

139
00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:45.950
In our example, from E we look at B

140
00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:47.230
which is already known

141
00:05:47.230 --> 00:05:49.350
and it is not E's parent.

142
00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:52.940
There is a cycle: B, C, E.

143
00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:56.188
In a directed graph the test is sharper

144
00:05:56.260 --> 00:05:58.271
an edge back to a vertex that

145
00:05:58.340 --> 00:06:00.100
is still being explored

146
00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:02.650
one that has started but not finished.

147
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:06.440
A tree, with no cycles, never triggers it.

148
00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:08.930
What does a search cost?

149
00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:12.450
Each vertex is taken off the frontier once.

150
00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:14.376
And each vertex's list of

151
00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:16.420
neighbours is read once.

152
00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:19.260
The lists hold twice the number of edges.

153
00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:21.459
In our example, twenty-two entries.

154
00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:24.242
So the total work is proportional to the number

155
00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:27.140
of vertices plus the number of edges.

156
00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:29.690
We write it order V plus E.

157
00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:31.906
Breadth-first and depth-first

158
00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:33.650
search have the same cost.

159
00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:36.880
They differ in the order, not in the effort.

160
00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:38.590
To sum up.

161
00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:40.670
Both searches keep a frontier.

162
00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:43.390
A queue gives breadth-first search

163
00:06:43.390 --> 00:06:45.490
with its rings and fewest edges.

164
00:06:45.490 --> 00:06:47.850
A stack, or recursion

165
00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:50.830
gives depth-first search, with its dives

166
00:06:50.830 --> 00:06:52.660
its times, and its cycles.

167
00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:55.220
Mark a vertex when you discover it

168
00:06:55.220 --> 00:06:58.290
and the cost is order V plus E.

169
00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:01.390
Next: edges with weights

170
00:07:01.390 --> 00:07:04.120
and the question of the cheapest route.

171
00:07:04.747 --> 00:07:06.947
Let's write both searches in Python.

172
00:07:07.527 --> 00:07:09.622
The frontier is a deque from

173
00:07:09.697 --> 00:07:10.866
the collections module.

174
00:07:10.917 --> 00:07:12.436
We keep the level of each

175
00:07:12.497 --> 00:07:13.915
vertex in a dictionary.

176
00:07:13.977 --> 00:07:15.971
A vertex is in that dictionary

177
00:07:16.037 --> 00:07:18.207
exactly when we have seen it.

178
00:07:18.557 --> 00:07:21.627
Take the oldest vertex with popleft.

179
00:07:21.627 --> 00:07:24.067
For each neighbour we have not seen

180
00:07:24.067 --> 00:07:26.807
record its level, remember its parent

181
00:07:26.807 --> 00:07:28.467
and put it on the queue.

182
00:07:28.467 --> 00:07:30.397
That is the marking step.

183
00:07:30.747 --> 00:07:33.217
We return the order, the levels

184
00:07:33.217 --> 00:07:34.627
and the parents.

185
00:07:35.207 --> 00:07:36.679
To recover a route

186
00:07:36.757 --> 00:07:38.267
start at the target and follow

187
00:07:38.317 --> 00:07:39.917
parents back to the start

188
00:07:39.917 --> 00:07:41.687
then reverse the list.

189
00:07:42.037 --> 00:07:45.907
For G we get A, B, E, G.

190
00:07:45.907 --> 00:07:48.997
Three edges, cost twenty-three, as we saw.

191
00:07:49.577 --> 00:07:52.519
Depth-first search is a recursive function.

192
00:07:52.587 --> 00:07:54.639
A clock counts up each time we

193
00:07:54.707 --> 00:07:57.097
discover or finish a vertex.

194
00:07:57.447 --> 00:07:59.618
On entry we record the discovery

195
00:07:59.677 --> 00:08:01.017
time and the order.

196
00:08:01.017 --> 00:08:03.637
We visit each neighbour we have not seen.

197
00:08:03.637 --> 00:08:06.127
On the way out we record the finish time.

198
00:08:06.477 --> 00:08:08.637
A nonlocal clock lets the inner

199
00:08:08.707 --> 00:08:10.557
function update the counter.

200
00:08:11.137 --> 00:08:14.007
The same search with an explicit stack

201
00:08:14.007 --> 00:08:15.267
a plain list.

202
00:08:15.267 --> 00:08:19.747
We pop a vertex, skip it if seen, mark it

203
00:08:19.747 --> 00:08:21.777
and push its unseen neighbours.

204
00:08:22.127 --> 00:08:24.678
The neighbours are pushed in reverse order

205
00:08:24.737 --> 00:08:26.550
so the alphabetically first

206
00:08:26.617 --> 00:08:28.274
one is popped first.

207
00:08:28.357 --> 00:08:30.490
Then it visits in the same order

208
00:08:30.557 --> 00:08:32.087
as the recursive version.

209
00:08:32.667 --> 00:08:34.657
Here is a real run.

210
00:08:34.657 --> 00:08:41.057
Breadth-first order: A, B, D, C, E, F, G.

211
00:08:41.057 --> 00:08:47.067
Depth-first order: A, B, C, E, D, F, G.

212
00:08:47.217 --> 00:08:48.688
The discovery and finish times

213
00:08:48.737 --> 00:08:50.427
are exactly the ones we drew.

214
00:08:51.007 --> 00:08:54.097
The classic bug is forgetting to mark.

215
00:08:54.097 --> 00:08:55.447
On a triangle

216
00:08:55.537 --> 00:08:57.595
after twenty pops the queue holds

217
00:08:57.657 --> 00:09:00.319
twenty-one vertices and A has been

218
00:09:00.397 --> 00:09:02.207
visited seven times.

219
00:09:02.557 --> 00:09:04.621
The other trap is depth.

220
00:09:04.707 --> 00:09:06.363
Python limits recursion to

221
00:09:06.427 --> 00:09:07.944
about a thousand frames

222
00:09:08.007 --> 00:09:10.797
so on a long chain the recursive version

223
00:09:10.867 --> 00:09:12.627
raises a recursion error.

224
00:09:12.627 --> 00:09:15.197
Use the stack version for big graphs.

225
00:09:15.777 --> 00:09:17.427
In the standard library

226
00:09:17.427 --> 00:09:19.210
use deque for a queue.

227
00:09:19.287 --> 00:09:20.541
Popping from the front of a

228
00:09:20.587 --> 00:09:22.167
list shifts every element

229
00:09:22.167 --> 00:09:24.527
which would make breadth-first search slow.

230
00:09:24.527 --> 00:09:26.737
A list is fine as a stack.

231
00:09:27.317 --> 00:09:30.787
So in Python: deque for the queue

232
00:09:30.787 --> 00:09:34.012
mark on discovery, and prefer the explicit

233
00:09:34.087 --> 00:09:36.197
stack on deep graphs.

234
00:09:36.547 --> 00:09:39.187
Next: Dijkstra's algorithm.

